[{"id":"29521","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"766","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span style=\"font-weight:bold\">Which of the following is an example of gas-solid foam type of colloid?</span></p>","option_1":"Milk","option_2":"Mist","option_3":"Milk of magnesia","option_4":"Sponge","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"4","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span>Correct Answer: Option D - Sponge.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span>A gas-solid foam is a colloid in which a gas is dispersed in a solid medium. Sponge (and similarly pumice stone) is the classic example, as gas bubbles are trapped within a solid matrix. Milk is a liquid-in-liquid emulsion, Mist is a liquid-in-gas aerosol, and Milk of magnesia is a solid-in-liquid suspension/sol - none of these are gas-solid foams.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"29522","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"766","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span style=\"font-weight:bold\">In India, what was the share of industrial sector in GDP in 1950-1951?</span></p>","option_1":"5%","option_2":"13%","option_3":"50%","option_4":"65%","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"2","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span>Correct Answer: Option B - 13%.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span>At the time of independence and in the early planning era (1950-51), India's economy was overwhelmingly agrarian. The industrial sector contributed only around 13% to the GDP, while agriculture accounted for the largest share. This composition changed significantly over subsequent Five-Year Plans as industrialisation was prioritised.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"29523","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"766","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span style=\"font-weight:bold\">Which one of the following pair is NOT correctly matched?</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span>I. The Panchayat – Part X</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span>II. Official Language – Part XVII</span></p>","option_1":"Only I","option_2":"Only II","option_3":"Both I and II","option_4":"Neither I Nor II","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"1","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span>Correct Answer: Option A - Only I.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span>In the Indian Constitution, Part IX deals with 'The Panchayats' (added by the 73rd Amendment), not Part X - Part X actually deals with 'The Scheduled and Tribal Areas'. Hence, statement I is incorrectly matched. Statement II is correct: Part XVII of the Constitution deals with 'Official Language'. Since only statement I is wrong, the pair NOT correctly matched is only I.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"29524","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"766","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span style=\"font-weight:bold\">The Coriolis force is responsible for deflecting winds towards the right in the northern hemisphere and towards the left in the southern hemisphere, this is known as _____.</span></p>","option_1":"Ferrel's Law","option_2":"Wagner's law","option_3":"Homes's law","option_4":"Davis's law","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"1","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span>Correct Answer: Option A - Ferrel's Law.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span>Ferrel's Law (named after American meteorologist William Ferrel) states that any object or fluid, including wind, moving freely over the earth's surface is deflected to the right in the Northern Hemisphere and to the left in the Southern Hemisphere due to the Coriolis effect caused by earth's rotation. This principle explains global wind patterns and ocean current directions.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"29525","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"766","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span style=\"font-weight:bold\">How many chambers are there in the heart of amphibians?</span></p>","option_1":"2","option_2":"3","option_3":"4","option_4":"5","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"2","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span>Correct Answer: Option B - 3.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span>The amphibian heart has three chambers - two atria (left and right) and one ventricle. This allows some mixing of oxygenated and deoxygenated blood in the single ventricle, unlike the four-chambered hearts of birds and mammals, which completely separate the two blood types.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"29526","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"766","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span style=\"font-weight:bold\">The term 'Samyakcharitra' in Jainism refers to _____.</span></p>","option_1":"Right faith","option_2":"Right conduct","option_3":"Right meditation","option_4":"Right knowledge","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"2","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span>Correct Answer: Option B - Right conduct.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span>In Jainism, the Triratna (Three Jewels) comprise Samyak Darshana (Right Faith), Samyak Gyana (Right Knowledge), and Samyak Charitra (Right Conduct) - together these constitute the path to liberation (moksha). 'Samyakcharitra' specifically denotes right conduct, involving ethical living based on principles like non-violence (ahimsa), truth, and non-attachment.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"29527","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"766","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span style=\"font-weight:bold\">Which of the following dances is traditionally performed in the state of Nagaland?</span></p>","option_1":"Shon","option_2":"Leshalaptu","option_3":"Phulpati","option_4":"Lezim","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"2","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span>Correct Answer: Option B - Leshalaptu.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span>Leshalaptu is a traditional folk dance of the Zeliang tribe of Nagaland, generally performed during harvest festivals to celebrate a good crop. Regional/tribal folk dances of the North-East are a recurring static GK topic in SSC exams.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"29528","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"766","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span style=\"font-weight:bold\">Which of the following organ secretes adrenaline in animals?</span></p>","option_1":"Pituitary gland","option_2":"Thyroid gland","option_3":"Adrenal gland","option_4":"Thymus gland","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"3","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span>Correct Answer: Option C - Adrenal gland.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span>Adrenaline (also called epinephrine) is secreted by the adrenal medulla, the inner part of the adrenal gland located above each kidney. It is released in response to stress, triggering the 'fight-or-flight' response. The pituitary gland is the 'master gland' regulating other endocrine glands, the thyroid secretes thyroxine, and the thymus is involved in immune system development - none of these secrete adrenaline.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"29529","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"766","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span style=\"font-weight:bold\">'Pushkar fair' is held in which of the following Indian state?</span></p>","option_1":"Rajasthan","option_2":"Kerala","option_3":"Punjab","option_4":"Bihar","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"1","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span>Correct Answer: Option A - Rajasthan.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span>The Pushkar Fair (Pushkar Camel Fair) is an annual livestock and cultural fair held in Pushkar, Rajasthan, known worldwide for camel and cattle trading along with cultural and religious festivities on the banks of Pushkar Lake.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"29530","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"766","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span style=\"font-weight:bold\">In 1600, which English Queen granted a charter to the East India Company, giving it exclusive trading rights with the East?</span></p>","option_1":"Queen Victoria","option_2":"Queen Mary","option_3":"Queen Anne","option_4":"Queen Elizabeth I","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"4","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span>Correct Answer: Option D - Queen Elizabeth I.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span>Queen Elizabeth I granted a Royal Charter on 31 December 1600 to a group of London merchants, forming the English East India Company and giving it a monopoly over English trade with the East Indies for 15 years. Queen Victoria reigned much later (1837-1901), during whose time India eventually became a formal British colony (the British Raj) after the Company's rule ended.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"29531","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"766","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span style=\"font-weight:bold\">In which Indian state is the 'Bushu' festival traditionally celebrated?</span></p>","option_1":"Bihar","option_2":"Punjab","option_3":"Nagaland","option_4":"Karnataka","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"3","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span>Correct Answer: Option C - Nagaland.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span>Bushu (or Bushu Jiba) is a post-harvest festival celebrated by the Chakhesang and Kuki tribes of Nagaland, marking the end of the agricultural cycle with feasting, dancing, and community celebrations.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"29532","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"766","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span style=\"font-weight:bold\">The Galaxies are spread over vast distances, which are measured in _____.</span></p>","option_1":"light years","option_2":"kilometres","option_3":"metres","option_4":"nanometres","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"1","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span>Correct Answer: Option A - light years.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span>Because of the enormous distances involved in astronomy, distances between galaxies and stars are measured in light years - the distance light travels in one Julian year (about 9.46 trillion km) - rather than in everyday units like kilometres or metres, which would be impractically small units for such vast cosmic scales.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"29533","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"766","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span style=\"font-weight:bold\">Which of the following incidents took place on 13 April 1919?</span></p>","option_1":"Ahmedabad Mill Workers Movement","option_2":"Jallianwala Bagh Massacre","option_3":"Chauri-Chaura incident","option_4":"Champaran Satyagraha Movement","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"2","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span>Correct Answer: Option B - Jallianwala Bagh Massacre.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span>The Jallianwala Bagh Massacre occurred on 13 April 1919 in Amritsar, Punjab, when British troops under General Reginald Dyer opened fire on a large peaceful crowd gathered at Jallianwala Bagh on Baisakhi day, killing hundreds of unarmed civilians. This event became a turning point in India's freedom struggle. The Champaran Satyagraha (1917), Ahmedabad Mill Workers' strike (1918), and Chauri-Chaura incident (1922) all occurred in different years.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"29534","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"766","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span style=\"font-weight:bold\">What is the name of the two spacecrafts involved in the Proba-3 mission?</span></p>","option_1":"SunSat and EarthSat","option_2":"Proba-A and Proba-B","option_3":"Ocra and Nexus","option_4":"Coronagraph and Occulter","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"4","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span>Correct Answer: Option D - Coronagraph and Occulter.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span>ESA's Proba-3 mission (launched December 2024) consists of two small satellites - the Coronagraph Spacecraft and the Occulter Spacecraft - that fly in precise formation to create an artificial solar eclipse, enabling detailed study of the Sun's faint corona by blocking out the Sun's bright disc using the Occulter satellite while the Coronagraph satellite captures images.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"29535","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"766","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span style=\"font-weight:bold\">As per the 5th report of National Family Health Survey (2019-21) the sex ratio of total population of India (females per 1000 males) is _____.</span></p>","option_1":"1020","option_2":"990","option_3":"950","option_4":"1080","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"1","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span>Correct Answer: Option A - 1020.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span>As per NFHS-5 (2019-21), the sex ratio of the total population of India stood at 1020 females per 1000 males, marking the first time since NFHS surveys began that the ratio favoured females at the national level - an improvement over NFHS-4 (2015-16), which had recorded 991 females per 1000 males.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"29536","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"766","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span style=\"font-weight:bold\">The kinetic energy of an object increases with its _____.</span></p>","option_1":"speed","option_2":"friction","option_3":"volume","option_4":"density","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"1","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span>Correct Answer: Option A - speed.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span>Kinetic energy is given by the formula KE = (1/2)mv², where m is mass and v is velocity/speed. Since KE is directly proportional to the square of the speed, an increase in speed leads to a (much larger) increase in kinetic energy. Friction, volume, and density do not directly determine kinetic energy in this formula.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"29537","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"766","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span style=\"font-weight:bold\">In March 2025, which country's government was accused of using a 'sonic weapon' against protestors, a device that emits painful sound waves?</span></p>","option_1":"Serbia","option_2":"Egypt","option_3":"Greece","option_4":"Madagascar","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"1","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span>Correct Answer: Option A - Serbia.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span>In March 2025, the Serbian government faced accusations of using a sonic weapon (a long-range acoustic device that emits painful, high-intensity sound waves) against large anti-government protestors in Belgrade, sparking controversy and denials from Serbian authorities.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"29538","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"766","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span style=\"font-weight:bold\">India aims to increase its wind energy capacity from 42 GW to 140 GW by _____.</span></p>","option_1":"2030","option_2":"2035","option_3":"2040","option_4":"2050","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"1","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span>Correct Answer: Option A - 2030.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span>As part of India's renewable energy and climate commitments, the government has set a target to expand wind energy capacity from around 42 GW to 140 GW by 2030, in line with the broader goal of achieving 500 GW of non-fossil fuel-based energy capacity by 2030.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"29539","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"766","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span style=\"font-weight:bold\">Which of the following port is located 105 Km downstream from Kolkata and has been constructed to reduce the congestion at Kolkata port?</span></p>","option_1":"Paradip","option_2":"Kandla","option_3":"Jawaharlal Nehru","option_4":"Haldia","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"4","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span>Correct Answer: Option D - Haldia.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span>Haldia Dock Complex, located about 105 km downstream from Kolkata on the Hooghly river, was developed as a satellite/subsidiary port of Kolkata Port to relieve congestion, since larger vessels found it difficult to navigate all the way up to Kolkata due to silting.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"29540","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"766","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span style=\"font-weight:bold\">Who is the author of the book 'In the Footsteps of Mahatma… Gandhi and Sanitation'?</span></p>","option_1":"Raja Rao","option_2":"Arun Shourie","option_3":"Sudarshan Iyengar","option_4":"Kamala Das","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"3","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span>Correct Answer: Option C - Sudarshan Iyengar.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span>'In the Footsteps of Mahatma: Gandhi and Sanitation' is authored by Sudarshan Iyengar, a Gandhian scholar and former Vice-Chancellor of Gujarat Vidyapith, exploring Gandhi's views and practices regarding sanitation and cleanliness as part of his broader social reform philosophy.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"29541","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"766","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span style=\"font-weight:bold\">All branches of Vijaya Bank and Dena Bank started to function as branches of Bank of Baroda from April 1, _____.</span></p>","option_1":"2019","option_2":"2021","option_3":"2023","option_4":"2017","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"1","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span>Correct Answer: Option A - 2019.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span>As part of the government's bank consolidation drive, Vijaya Bank and Dena Bank were amalgamated with Bank of Baroda, with the merger taking effect from 1 April 2019, creating the country's third largest bank at the time.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"29542","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"766","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span style=\"font-weight:bold\">According to the laws of table tennis, a player can win a game of table tennis by scoring _____ points (if there is no tie at 10-10).</span></p>","option_1":"11","option_2":"9","option_3":"12","option_4":"15","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"1","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span>Correct Answer: Option A - 11.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span>Under the current ITTF (International Table Tennis Federation) rules, a game is won by the first player to score 11 points, provided there is a lead of at least 2 points; if the score reaches 10-10 (deuce), play continues until one player leads by 2 points.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"29543","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"766","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span style=\"font-weight:bold\">Who built 'Dinpanah' on the mound of ancient Indraprastha?</span></p>","option_1":"Bakhtiyar Khilji","option_2":"Akbar","option_3":"Humayun","option_4":"Mohammed Bin Tughlaq","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"3","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span>Correct Answer: Option C - Humayun.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span>Dinpanah ('Refuge of Faith/Religion') was a fortified city built by the Mughal emperor Humayun on the mound believed to be the site of ancient Indraprastha, along the Yamuna river. It was later expanded and is now known as Purana Qila (Old Fort) in Delhi.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"29544","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"766","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span style=\"font-weight:bold\">Which of the following is a Tat Vadya?</span></p>","option_1":"Para","option_2":"Dandia","option_3":"Dilruba","option_4":"Flute","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"3","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span>Correct Answer: Option C - Dilruba.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span>In Indian musicology, musical instruments are classified into four categories: Tat Vadya (stringed instruments), Sushir Vadya (wind instruments), Avanaddh Vadya (percussion/membranophones), and Ghan Vadya (solid/idiophones). Dilruba is a bowed, stringed instrument and hence a Tat Vadya. Flute is a Sushir Vadya (wind instrument). This question was left unanswered by the candidate.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"29545","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"766","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span style=\"font-weight:bold\">Which country is ranked 1st in the Global Firepower Index 2025 with the lowest Power Index score of 0.0744?</span></p>","option_1":"USA","option_2":"Russia","option_3":"China","option_4":"India","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"1","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span>Correct Answer: Option A - USA.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span>As per the Global Firepower Index 2025 (where a lower Power Index score indicates a stronger military), the United States of America ranked 1st with a score of 0.0744, retaining its position as the world's most powerful military as per this index, ahead of Russia, China, and India. This question was left unanswered by the candidate.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""}]